<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="ch7-section-total-energy-equation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Total energy equation</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We can sum the thermodynamic energy equation <xref ref="eqn-thermodyanmic-energy-equation" text="type-global" />
      and the mechanical energy equation <xref ref="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation" text="type-global" />
      to express the time rate of change of an air parcel's total energy.
    </p>

    <p>
      Following the derivation presented in <xref ref="martin" text="custom">Martin (2006)</xref>,
      we first rearrange the thermodynamic energy equation
      <xref ref="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation" text="type-global" />
      so its terms sum to 0:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md xml:id="eqn-mechanical-energy-equation-zero-sum" number="yes">
          \frac{D}{Dt} \left(
          \frac{u^2 + v^2 + w^2}{2}
          + \Phi
          \right)
          +
          \frac{1}{\rho} \vec{u} \cdot \nabla p
          -
          \nu \vec{u} \cdot \nabla^2 \vec{u}
          =
          0
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Then we add this quantity to the thermodynamic energy equation
      <xref ref="eqn-thermodyanmic-energy-equation" text="type-global" />.
      This is equivalent to adding 0 to <xref ref="eqn-thermodyanmic-energy-equation" text="type-global" />,
      so its value is unchanged.
    </p>

    <p>
      <md xml:id="eqn-total-energy-equation-start" number="yes">
          \dot{Q}
          =
          c_v \frac{DT}{Dt}
          +
          p \frac{D \alpha}{Dt}
          + \frac{D}{Dt} \left(
          \frac{u^2 + v^2 + w^2}{2}
          + \Phi
          \right)
          +
          \frac{1}{\rho} \vec{u} \cdot \nabla p
          -
          \nu \vec{u} \cdot \nabla^2 \vec{u}
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      The negative of the pressure work term expands as follows:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
          <mrow>
              \frac{1}{\rho} \vec{u} \cdot \nabla p
              \amp =
              \alpha \left(
                  u \frac{\partial p}{\partial x}
                  +
                  v \frac{\partial p}{\partial y}
                  +
                  w \frac{\partial p}{\partial z}
              \right)
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
              \amp =
              \alpha \left(
                  \frac{Dp}{Dt}
                  -
                  \frac{\partial p}{\partial t}
              \right)
          </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Since
    </p>

    <p>
      <md number="yes">
          \frac{D (p \alpha)}{Dt}
          =
          p \frac{D \alpha}{Dt}
          +
          \alpha \frac{Dp}{Dt}
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      and
    </p>

    <p>
      <md number="yes">
          c_v \frac{DT}{Dt} = \frac{D(c_v T)}{Dt}
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      since <m>c_v</m> can be treated as constant,
      it follows that <xref ref="eqn-total-energy-equation-start" text="type-global" /> becomes
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>
          \dot{Q}
          =
          \frac{D (c_v T)}{Dt}
          +
          \frac{D (p \alpha)}{Dt}
          + \frac{D}{Dt} \left(
          \frac{u^2 + v^2 + w^2}{2}
          + \Phi
          \right)
          -
          \alpha \frac{\partial p}{\partial t}
          -
          \nu \vec{u} \cdot \nabla^2 \vec{u}
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      which can be written more simply as
    </p>

    <p>
      <md xml:id="eqn-total-energy-equation" number="yes">
          \dot{Q}
          =
          \frac{D}{Dt} \left(
              \frac{u^2 + v^2 + w^2}{2}
              + \Phi
              + c_v T
              + p \alpha
          \right)
          -
          \alpha \frac{\partial p}{\partial t}
          -
          \nu \vec{u} \cdot \nabla^2 \vec{u}
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      by combining all terms that have the total derivative <m>\dfrac{D}{Dt}</m> applied to them.
    </p>

    <p>
      <xref ref="eqn-total-energy-equation" text="type-global" />
      is the <term>total energy equation</term>
      (or simply the <term>energy equation</term>).
    </p>

    <p>
      See <xref ref="holton-hakim" text="custom">Holton and Hakim (2013)</xref>
      for an alternative derivation with viscous friction effects neglected.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <xi:include href="ch7-section-total-energy-equation-subsection-bernoulli.ptx" />

</section>